The recursive version compares the current characters from the two strings. A match adds one and moves both positions. A mismatch tries skipping one character from either string.

The same pair of positions can appear many times, so the result is saved in `memo`. Empty strings return zero naturally.

```java
class Solution {
    private int[][] memo;

    public int lcs(String str1, String str2) {
        int n = str1.length();
        int m = str2.length();
        memo = new int[n + 1][m + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            java.util.Arrays.fill(memo[i], -1);
        }

        return solve(str1, str2, 0, 0);
    }

    private int solve(String str1, String str2, int i, int j) {
        if (i == str1.length() || j == str2.length()) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (memo[i][j] != -1) {
            return memo[i][j];
        }

        if (str1.charAt(i) == str2.charAt(j)) {
            memo[i][j] = 1 + solve(str1, str2, i + 1, j + 1);
        } else {
            int skipFirst = solve(str1, str2, i + 1, j);
            int skipSecond = solve(str1, str2, i, j + 1);
            memo[i][j] = Math.max(skipFirst, skipSecond);
        }

        return memo[i][j];
    }
}
```

A small dry run: for `"abc"` and `"ac"`, `a` matches first. Later `b` and `c` differ, so the recursion skips one side and finds `ac`, giving `2`.

If either input is empty, the method returns `0`. The table uses extra memory, but it avoids the repeated work of plain recursion.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * m)` for the table, plus `O(n + m)` for the call stack.

A full matrix is not necessary because one row only needs values from the row just before it. Two arrays make that relationship clear and avoid the extra care needed by a single in-place array.

`previous` describes answers after processing an earlier character of `str1`. `current` is filled for the next character, then the arrays are exchanged.

```javascript
class Solution {
    lcs(str1, str2) {
        // Keep the second array dimension small.
        if (str1.length < str2.length) {
            const temporary = str1;
            str1 = str2;
            str2 = temporary;
        }

        const width = str2.length + 1;
        let previous = new Array(width).fill(0);

        for (let i = 1; i <= str1.length; i++) {
            const current = new Array(width).fill(0);

            for (let j = 1; j <= str2.length; j++) {
                if (str1[i - 1] === str2[j - 1]) {
                    current[j] = previous[j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    current[j] = Math.max(previous[j], current[j - 1]);
                }
            }

            previous = current;
        }

        return previous[str2.length];
    }
}
```

For `str1 = "abc"` and `str2 = "def"`, no comparison matches, so every row stays filled with zero. Empty strings also work because the initial row already represents an empty string.

The two arrays are simpler to inspect than a single array, although one array can reduce the constant memory a little more.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`.
Space complexity: `O(min(n, m))`.

A table makes the transitions easy to follow. `dp[i][j]` stores the answer for the first `i` characters of `str1` and the first `j` characters of `str2`.

When the characters match, the diagonal value is extended. When they differ, the larger value from above or from the left is kept.

```cpp
class Solution {
  public:
    int lcs(string str1, string str2) {
        int n = str1.size();
        int m = str2.size();
        vector<vector<int>> dp(n + 1, vector<int>(m + 1, 0));

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++) {
                if (str1[i - 1] == str2[j - 1]) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[n][m];
    }
};
```

For example, with `str1 = "abcde"` and `str2 = "ace"`, the matching characters build the answer `3` through `a`, `c`, and `e`.

Time complexity: `O(n * m)`.
Space complexity: `O(n * m)`.

This is the direct recursive version. When the last characters match, both are used. Otherwise, the code removes one last character at a time and keeps the larger result.

It is useful for checking the recurrence, but it repeats many calls. It should not be used for large strings because the running time grows quickly.

```go
func lcs(str1, str2 string) int {
    return solveLCS(str1, str2, len(str1), len(str2))
}

func solveLCS(str1, str2 string, firstLength, secondLength int) int {
    if firstLength == 0 || secondLength == 0 {
        return 0
    }

    if str1[firstLength-1] == str2[secondLength-1] {
        return 1 + solveLCS(str1, str2, firstLength-1, secondLength-1)
    }

    withoutFirst := solveLCS(str1, str2, firstLength-1, secondLength)
    withoutSecond := solveLCS(str1, str2, firstLength, secondLength-1)

    if withoutFirst > withoutSecond {
        return withoutFirst
    }
    return withoutSecond
}
```

An edge case is when the strings contain no common character, such as `"abc"` and `"XYZ"`. Every branch eventually reaches zero, so the result is `0`. Two identical strings return their full length.

Time complexity: exponential in the worst case, commonly written as `O(2^(n + m))`.
Space complexity: `O(n + m)` for the recursion stack.

